<section id="e3" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">4.3</span>The exclusion rule and the flat numbers</h2>
<ol>
<li>The two old "Why" sentences move to the record's history, under your open answer. The first called 2009 prelude code "the team's last record of the Fortress way", with a phrase you had said about the rule in the team's 2012 patent applications. The second joined the two meanings of specialisation; you wrote under it that it wasn't an answer.</li>
<li>New, from the judgement, for your yes: the grounds in the language. The exclusion rule says that no type extends one generic trait at two different type arguments. The team states it in four texts, from 2011 to 2019. Under it, a nested tower is impossible: if <code>ZZ32 extends ZZ64</code>, then <code>ZZ32</code> is <code>Integral</code> at its own type and at <code>ZZ64</code>'s. And the Working Draft's own types chapter says that the number types "are mutually exclusive".</li>
<li>New, from the judgement, for your yes: the prelude's flat numbers change role, from the reason to the team's instance of the same answer on the compiled path.</li>
<li>New, from the judgement, for your yes: a return to route C, a nested tower, is weighed against its measured price and against the entry that keeps it open. The list of flat number types is now written out; that changes no meaning.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The new text</h3>
<div class="entry-text"><p>P-43 <strong>The exclusion rule stays, and the number tower is flat (route A).</strong> The compiled checker keeps the multiple instantiation exclusion rule: no type extends two different instantiations of one generic trait. So the library's numbers are flat. The fixed-width types (<code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>NN32</code>, <code>NN64</code>, <code>RR32</code>, <code>RR64</code>), <code>ZZ</code> and <code>QQ</code> are siblings under <code>Number</code>, each with its own algebra, and <code>Number</code> has no catch-all operators. The grounds are in the language (P-26 defines the layers). The type group states the rule in four texts: the 2011 paper (<code>Papers/Types/exclusion.tick</code>), the 2012 Types chapter, Karl Naden's 2012 write-up and the POPL 2019 paper. Under the rule a nested tower whose every level carries the algebra traits at its own type is impossible: if <code>ZZ32 extends ZZ64</code>, then <code>ZZ32</code> is <code>Integral</code> at two instantiations. The Working Draft's types chapter says that the numeric types "are mutually exclusive; no value has more than one of them" (<code>Specification-1.0-frozen/basic/types-vals-vars.tex</code>). The later texts side with that sentence against the Working Draft's library chapters, which make ℤ a subtype of ℚ (P-26). The compiler's prelude, flattened by Chase, Ryu, Maessen and Allen in 2009 and 2010, is the team's instance of the same answer on the compiled path, not a ground for it. Route C is a nested tower under the forest rule of the team's 2012 patent applications. Weigh any return to it against F-46, which gives what it would need, and P-95, which keeps it open.</p></div>
<details class="old"><summary>The old text</summary><p>P-43 <strong>The exclusion rule stays, and the number tower is flat (route A).</strong> The compiled checker keeps the multiple instantiation exclusion rule. So the library's numbers are flat, as the team (Chase, Ryu, Maessen and Allen) flattened the compiler prelude in 2009 and 2010. The fixed widths, <code>ZZ</code>, <code>QQ</code> and <code>RR64</code> are siblings under <code>Number</code>, each with its own algebra, and <code>Number</code> has no catch-all operators. Why: the flattened prelude is the team's last record of the Fortress way, and its route leads to the POPL 2019 paper, which keeps the rule. He cares that specialised code runs fast, not how a specialisation is chosen. F-46 prices a return to route C.</p></details>
