<section id="e4" class="part">
<h2><span class="num">4.4</span>The default bound is <code>Any</code></h2>
<ol>
<li>The bound of a type parameter is the largest type it may stand for. "Functions" is dropped from what <code>Any</code> holds, under your open answer. The team's texts disagree on functions and agree on tuples. The title, your decision, is unchanged.</li>
<li>New, from the judgement, for your yes: the grounds in the language. The 2019 paper says the default bound is <code>Any</code>. The Working Draft says type parameters take tuple types, which its own <code>Object</code> sentence would forbid, and that sentence isn't in the 2008 release. The library fills its type parameters with tuples, as <code>zip</code> does.</li>
<li>New, from the judgement, for your yes: Steele's 2012 setting enforces the draft's sentence on the compiled path alone; it is not the language's rule. The old text said the switch-over ends the <code>Object</code> bound. It doesn't: one line of <code>Shell.java</code> does. Under that line, some calls also compile wrong.</li>
<li>New: two failures of the compiled path that don't depend on the bound: <code>()</code> as a static argument, the type that fills a type parameter, and a generic method, called with a dot, whose one parameter has a tuple type. And, from the judgement, for your yes: Java gives no ground for <code>Object</code>, since Java's own <code>Object</code> maps to Fortress <code>Any</code>.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The new text</h3>
<div class="entry-text"><p>P-42 <strong>A type parameter declared without a bound has the implicit bound <code>Any</code>, not <code>Object</code>.</strong> <code>Any</code> holds tuples and <code>()</code>, which <code>Object</code> does not. The grounds are in the language (P-26 defines the layers). The POPL 2019 paper says "By default, each type parameter has an upper bound Any". The Working Draft says that type parameters are instantiated with tuple and arrow types, which its own <code>Object</code> default would forbid, and its <code>Object</code> sentence is not in the 1.0 release of 2008. The library declares its type parameters without a bound and instantiates them at tuple types, as in <code>zip</code>'s <code>(E,F)</code> and its arrays indexed by pairs <code>(ZZ32,ZZ32)</code>. The specification states the bound <code>Any</code>, with a callout that records that the Working Draft of February 2011 said <code>Object</code>. In the team's implementation, Steele's setting of 2012 gives the compiled path the bound <code>Object</code>: it enforces the Working Draft's sentence on that path alone, and it is not the language's rule. <code>fortress compile</code> still sets it (<code>Shell.java:372</code>, in <code>useCompilerLibraries()</code>), and so refuses a generic instantiated at a tuple (row 412), or in some calls compiles it wrong (row 691). The switch-over does not end this by itself; that one line does. On the compiled path, two failures do not depend on the bound: <code>()</code> as a static argument (row 339), and a generic dotted method with one tuple-typed parameter (row 832). Java interoperability gives no ground for <code>Object</code>: <code>java.lang.Object</code> maps to Fortress <code>Any</code> (<code>NamingCzar.java:447</code>).</p></div>
<details class="old"><summary>The old text</summary><p>P-42 <strong>A type parameter declared without a bound has the implicit bound <code>Any</code>, not <code>Object</code>.</strong> <code>Any</code> holds tuples, functions and <code>()</code>, which <code>Object</code> does not. The library instantiates its generic containers at tuple types: its arrays indexed by pairs, such as <code>(ZZ32,ZZ32)</code>, are among them. The specification states the bound <code>Any</code>, with a callout that records that the Working Draft of February 2011 said <code>Object</code>. The compiled path departs from it until the switch-over, which ends the departure: while it uses the compiler's prelude, it bounds such a parameter by <code>Object</code>, and so refuses a generic instantiated at a tuple (row 412).</p></details>
